and the outings are organized entirely by adults.

257
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:51,000
What I want to know is how can I help change this?

258
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:55,000
How somebody helps change the way that a troupe functions

259
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:57,000
is a pretty common question,

260
00:12:57,000 --> 00:13:00,000
And the most direct, simple answer I have for you

261
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:04,000
is that there are three roles organizationally

262
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:08,000
within a scout troupe that are empowered to make changes.

263
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:10,000
That's the chartered organization representative,

264
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:11,000
the troupe committee chairman

265
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:14,000
and, to a lesser extent, the scout master.

266
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:17,000
They're the only people who have the organizational role

267
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:21,000
that empowers them to make changes like you would like to see.

268
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:24,000
Can someone who's not occupying one of those roles

269
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:28,000
help make changes to the way that a troupe functions?

270
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:31,000
Well, you know, naturally, that all depends

271
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:34,000
on how receptive the people in those roles are

272
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:36,000
to change and suggestion,

273
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:39,000
And I will tell you that, unfortunately, in my experience,

274
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:43,000
about eight or nine out of ten times they're not interested

275
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:47,000
And pressing these ideas becomes onerous to them

276
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:52,000
and subsequently you are identified as a troublemaker.

277
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:54,000
So where do we go from here?

278
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:57,000
Well, your scout's interests individually

279
00:13:57,000 --> 00:14:01,000
is the most important factor in determining your way forward. okay,

280
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:03,000
Let's not kid ourselves, folks.

281
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:07,000
Many, many troops are administered just as described

282
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:09,000
and scouts get something out of being a part of them.

283
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:12,000
They get something out of scouting in that experience.

284
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:14,000
That's undeniable.

285
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:17,000
So if your scout is happy,

286
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:19,000
if he's built a lot of friendships within the troupe

287
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:22,000
and you make your suggestions

288
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:25,000
or you share your ideas with how things ought to change

289
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:27,000
and those are rejected,

290
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:30,000
then I would say, in the interest of your scout

291
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:33,000
and because he's built up friendships in the troupe,

292
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:35,000
he's getting something out of the program,

293
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:37,000
just leave well enough alone.

294
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:39,000
Now, if your scout is dissatisfied

295
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:41,000
with the way things are going

296
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:44,000
or you as a parent, weigh the importance

297
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:46,000
of having him participate in a troupe

298
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:48,000
that uses the patrol system,

299
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:50,000
if you weigh that very heavily,

300
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:54,000
then you're going to need to find another troupe.

301
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:57,000
You know, I know that that's not what most people want to hear,

302
00:14:57,000 --> 00:14:59,000
but it's actually that simple.

303
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:01,000
There's lots of stories of adults

304
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:03,000
who try to encourage change in an unwilling troupe

305
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:05,000
and if they keep pressing things

306
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:07,000
or they try and make an issue out of it,

307
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:10,000
it all ends up with a lot of hard feelings.

308
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:12,000
So I would strongly suggest

309
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:14,000
you find a troupe that is more suited

310
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:16,000
to what you want for your scout.

311
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:19,000
If you aren't in a role to make the changes

312
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:21,000
like I described before,

313
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:23,000
there's really no other viable solution.

314
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:26,000
Now the next concern that is usually raised

315
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:28,000
when I have these discussions with people

316
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:31,000
is: how can I in good conscience

317
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:33,000
abandon the other scouts you know

318
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:35,000
to this troupe that doesn't get it

319
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:37,000
and who's running a program?

320
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:39,000
that's not the patrol system

321
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:42,000
and it's not what Clark talks about.

322
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:44,000
Now, if you decide that your family

323
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:46,000
is going to change troops

324
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:48,000
and other parents ask you why,

325
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:50,000
well then you can share the reasons with them

326
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:52,000
and maybe they will decide

327
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:54,000
to make a similar change.

328
00:15:54,000 --> 00:15:56,000
If enough of them do this,

329
00:15:56,000 --> 00:15:58,000
folks in the troupe that you left

330
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:00,000
may get the message.

331
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:02,000
Unfortunately they may not.

332
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:05,000
Let's remember what's important here.

333
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:09,000
The interest of your scout is important.

334
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:11,000
If he's happy, satisfied,

335
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:13,000
if he's got a lot of friendships in the troupe

336
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:14,000
and everything like that,

337
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:16,000
I would strongly urge you

338
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:20,000
that if your ideas and suggestions are rejected,

339
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:22,000
that you just leave well enough alone,

340
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:24,000
because it's in his best interest.

341
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:26,000
If he's dissatisfied

342
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:28,000
and he's ready to make a change,

343
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:30,000
get it done.

344
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:32,000
It's a really difficult situation.

345
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:34,000
It's a very common situation

346
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:36,000
but it's very, very difficult

347
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:39,000
and I hope that advice helps.

348
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:41,000
Heard from Jeff Crump,

349
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:43,000
who is the scout master

350
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:46,000
of troupe 701 in Wakefield, Massachusetts.

351
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:48,000
He said in September

352
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:50,000
I was chosen as the newest scout master

353
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:51,000
of our troupe.

354
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:53,000
I love your podcast and blog

355
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:55,000
and you can help me immensely

356
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:57,000
as I've been trying to figure all this out.

357
00:16:57,000 --> 00:17:00,000
We're all still trying to figure it all out,

358
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:02,000
Doesn't matter how long we've been around, Jeff.

359
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:04,000
My question revolves around

360
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:06,000
how much should a scout master

361
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:09,000
be involved with Eagle paperwork?

362
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:11,000
I had a great scout master conference

363
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:13,000
with an Eagle candidate recently,

364
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:15,000
but when he submitted his paperwork

365
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:18,000
the essay was returned to him

366
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:22,000
by our council as being insufficient.

367
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:24,000
Some folks were upset with me

368
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:26,000
because I didn't proofread the essay

369
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:28,000
or review the application

370
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:30,000
during the scout master conference

371
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:32,000
and they implied that part of the scout master's job

372
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:34,000
is to make sure that all the eyes are dotted

373
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:36,000
and the T's are crossed.

374
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:38,000
My feeling is, if the scout

375
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:40,000
wanted any help with his essay,

376
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:42,000
he could have asked me,

377
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:44,000
and that the scout master conference

378
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:46,000
is really not the place to review paperwork.

379
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:48,000
We have Eagle mentors that help

380
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:50,000
scouts navigate the whole process.

381
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:52,000
As I said, in the recent past

382
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:54,000
some of these adults have been pulling scouts

383
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:56,000
over the finish line a little too aggressively.

384
00:17:56,000 --> 00:17:58,000
What do you think the proper level

385
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:00,000
of involvement is for the scout master

386
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:02,000
and Eagle mentors?

387
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:04,000
Well, first off, Jeff, I noticed that you became a backer

388
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:06,000
and I wanted to say thank you so much for that.

389
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:08,000
and I'm always heartened to hear

390
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:10,000
I'm providing some assistance

391
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:12,000
to my fellow scouters out there.

392
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:14,000
and you know I'll tell you

393
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:16,000
that being a scout master is great fun,

394
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:18,000
Even though we run into problems

395
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:20,000
like this now and again.

396
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:22,000
but for the most part, you know, it's a great job.

397
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:24,000
and in my experience

398
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:26,000
there's no more kind of overblown, overmanaged

399
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:28,000
cause of problems

400
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:30,000
that scout masters encounter

401
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:32,000
than all of this kind of

402
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:34,000
weirdness and frustration surrounding

403
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:36,000
Eagle Scout.

404
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:38,000
It has

405
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:40,000
kind of the highest freak out factor

406
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:42,000
of anything else in scouting

407
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:44,000
as far as my experience goes.

408
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:46,000
First, to clear up a couple of

409
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:48,000
terminology things,

410
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:50,000
and let's just clear that up real quick:

411
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:52,000
There's no essay required for Eagle.

412
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:54,000
there's a statement of

413
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:56,000
ambitions and life purpose that accompanies the application

414
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:58,000
and

415
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:00,000
we have project coaches.

416
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:02,000
we don't have Eagle mentors.

417
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:04,000
really, The Eagle mentor thing

418
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:06,000
is a pin

419
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:08,000
that an Eagle Scout can present

420
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:10,000
to someone who

421
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:12,000
he considers has been influential

422
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:14,000
in helping him reach his goal.

423
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:16,000
My simple answer to your question-

424
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:18,000
what is your proper level

425
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:20,000
involvement and the proper level

426
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:22,000
involvement for project coaches-

427
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:24,000
is easy

428
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:26,000
because I would answer

429
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:28,000
as little as possible

430
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:30,000
and, most of the time, only when

431
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:32,000
the candidate requests our help.

432
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:34,000
The best advice I have

433
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:36,000
for anybody working with Eagle candidates

434
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:38,000
is to take a good long look

435
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:40,000
at the applicable

436
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:42,000
section of the Guide to Advancement 2013,

437
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:44,000
the Eagle Application

438
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:46,000
and the Eagle Project Workbook.

439
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:48,000
once you have a working

440
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:50,000
knowledge of the process,

441
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:52,000
you'll better understand what your role is

442
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:54,000
and you can aid Scouts

443
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:56,000
in navigating their way through it.

444
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,000
The Eagle process to find in those documents

445
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:00,000
is actually very simple

446
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:02,000
and most of the verbiage

447
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:04,000
in the documents-

448
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:06,000
check them out and see if you agree with me-

449
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:08,000
is there to protect Scouts

450
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:10,000
from the kind of capricious, over anxious

451
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:12,000
interference that we sometimes see.

452
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:14,000
Now, unfortunately, I think

453
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:16,000
it grows to be a habit that

454
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:18,000
adults perform

455
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:20,000
really intensive reviews of

456
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:22,000
the paperwork and the Scouts

457
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:24,000
writing and stuff on

458
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:26,000
all of these forms and things

459
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:28,000
and they review it pretty intensively

460
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:30,000
and that leads to a lot of polishing

461
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:32,000
and expanding and rewriting

462
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:34,000
on the Scouts part. so

463
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:36,000
I guess if

464
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:38,000
a statement or

465
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:40,000
report actually written by the Scouts,

466
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:42,000
somehow slips through.

467
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:44,000
people are going to read it

468
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:46,000
and they might look at it and say,

469
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:48,000
well, this is insufficient

470
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:50,000
because no adults have been involved

471
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:52,000
in creating it. There's no

472
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:54,000
essay required for Eagle. What you're

473
00:20:54,000 --> 00:20:56,000
referring to is called

474
00:20:56,000 --> 00:20:58,000
a statement of ambitions and life

475
00:20:58,000 --> 00:21:00,000
purpose in requirement 7

476
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:02,000
that accompanies the Eagle Application.

477
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:04,000
That requirement does not

478
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:06,000
define a certain length for

479
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:08,000
that statement or anything

480
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:10,000
that would indicate to me it could be judged

481
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:12,000
insufficient, let alone that

482
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:14,000
anyone's even really kind of permitted

483
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:16,000
to make that judgment. But anyway,

484
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:18,000
so far as the Scoutmasters

485
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:20,000
role in all this, well, my habit

486
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:22,000
of Scoutmaster was to review the workbook

487
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:24,000
briefly with the Scout

488
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:26,000
during his life Scoutmaster conference

489
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:28,000
and make sure he understood

490
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:30,000
what the project workbook

491
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:32,000
was so that he could get himself

492
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:34,000
rolling on beginning thinking

493
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:36,000
about an Eagle project.

494
00:21:36,000 --> 00:21:38,000
Now, once a Scouts decided,

495
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:40,000
yeah, I'm going to be an Eagle Scout,

496
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:42,000
I'll do my best to offer help

497
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:44,000
and answer questions and make suggestions,

498
00:21:44,000 --> 00:21:46,000
but 7 or 8 times out of 10

499
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:48,000
I leave well enough alone.

500
00:21:48,000 --> 00:21:50,000
If he's told me he wants to be an Eagle Scout

501
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:52,000
and we go a couple months and there doesn't seem

502
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:54,000
to be any movement, you know, I'm going to ask him about it.

503
00:21:54,000 --> 00:21:56,000
I'm going to encourage him,

504
00:21:56,000 --> 00:21:58,000
I'm going to do what I can,

505
00:21:58,000 --> 00:22:00,000
but the next time I get officially involved

506
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:02,000
as a Scoutmaster

507
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:04,000
is reviewing the project proposal

508
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:06,000
because it requires my signature,

509
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:08,000
I may look at it and we may

510
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:10,000
discuss what a proposal is,

511
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:12,000
what a plan is. you know

512
00:22:12,000 --> 00:22:14,000
some notes about presenting it

513
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:16,000
to the troop committee

514
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:18,000
and what I found is that

515
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:20,000
many, many times Scouts have never

516
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:22,000
written a proposal

517
00:22:22,000 --> 00:22:24,000
and they may need a little help

518
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:26,000
defining exactly how that works.

519
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:28,000
so after I've done that,

520
00:22:28,000 --> 00:22:30,000
I really don't have an official

521
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:32,000
role until I end up at the Scoutmaster

522
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:34,000
conference for Eagle

523
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:36,000
and we talk about what his plans are

524
00:22:36,000 --> 00:22:38,000
and what he'd like to do in his future.

525
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:40,000
usually by this time he's got

526
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:42,000
the application- the Eagle application-

527
00:22:42,000 --> 00:22:44,000
in his hands and it's filled out.

528
00:22:44,000 --> 00:22:46,000
he's written his statement.

529
00:22:46,000 --> 00:22:48,000
but I rarely read the statements because

530
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:50,000
we're sitting there talking about it anyway

531
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:52,000
and I'd much rather talk to the Scout

532
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:54,000
about these things than read it off of a piece of paper.

533
00:22:54,000 --> 00:22:56,000
I've had somewhere north

534
00:22:56,000 --> 00:22:58,000
of 80 and south of 110

535
00:22:58,000 --> 00:23:00,000
Eagle Scouts of Scoutmaster

536
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:02,000
and probably have read

537
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:04,000
maybe 10 or 15 statements because

538
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:06,000
the Scout asked my opinion of it.

539
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:08,000
in each case I kind of scan what

540
00:23:08,000 --> 00:23:10,000
they write and say it looks good to me,

541
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:12,000
because in a lot of ways

542
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:14,000
it's really none of my business.

543
00:23:14,000 --> 00:23:16,000
this is his work,

544
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:18,000
this is what he's sending forward,

545
00:23:18,000 --> 00:23:20,000
and I can't ever recall

546
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:22,000
looking at a statement that I would have judged insufficient,

547
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:24,000
and then, basically,

548
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:26,000
my last official

549
00:23:26,000 --> 00:23:28,000
in all this is shaking the Scout's hand

550
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:30,000
and handing him an Eagle badge.

551
00:23:30,000 --> 00:23:32,000
anyone else involved with this process?

552
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:34,000
you know the advancement chair is going to get involved.

553
00:23:34,000 --> 00:23:36,000
you can have a project coach,

554
00:23:36,000 --> 00:23:38,000
you can do all kinds of things.

555
00:23:38,000 --> 00:23:40,000
I think the Eagle process is

556
00:23:40,000 --> 00:23:42,000
really very simple.

557
00:23:42,000 --> 00:23:44,000
I think that it should be kept simple

558
00:23:44,000 --> 00:23:46,000
and I think that people shouldn't get quite so anxious

559
00:23:46,000 --> 00:23:48,000
about some of the things that they get anxious about.

560
00:23:48,000 --> 00:23:50,000
and I'll say this,

561
00:23:50,000 --> 00:23:52,000
and I think I've said it many times:

562
00:23:52,000 --> 00:23:54,000
there's only one way for a Scout to reach Eagle:

563
00:23:54,000 --> 00:23:56,000
completes all the requirements

564
00:23:56,000 --> 00:23:58,000
right and then he successfully passes

565
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:00,000
a duly appointed Board of Review.

566
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:02,000
there's no Eagle Plus,

567
00:24:02,000 --> 00:24:04,000
there's no Eagle Minus,

568
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:06,000
there's only Eagle. and everything else is kind of window dressing,

569
00:24:06,000 --> 00:24:08,000
isn't it?

570
00:24:08,000 --> 00:24:10,000
Eagle means something different to each Scout.

571
00:24:10,000 --> 00:24:12,000
each one of them is an individual.

572
00:24:12,000 --> 00:24:14,000
each one of them is different.

573
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:16,000
Jeff got back and said:

574
00:24:16,000 --> 00:24:18,000
thanks for the response.

575
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:20,000
I do appreciate it.

576
00:24:20,000 --> 00:24:22,000
I agree, the process can get a little overblown,

577
00:24:22,000 --> 00:24:24,000
and you're right, I was referring to the statement

578
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:26,000
of ambitions and life purpose.

579
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:28,000
thanks for your advice and all your hard work

580
00:24:28,000 --> 00:24:30,000
on the website and the podcast.

581
00:24:30,000 --> 00:24:32,000
Brad Schroer is the

582
00:24:32,000 --> 00:24:34,000
Scout Master, Troop 414

583
00:24:34,000 --> 00:24:36,000
in Bedford, New Hampshire,

584
00:24:36,000 --> 00:24:38,000
said I've listed quite a few of your podcasts.

585
00:24:38,000 --> 00:24:40,000
thanks for all you do.

586
00:24:40,000 --> 00:24:42,000
I've been moving our troop along

587
00:24:42,000 --> 00:24:44,000
with some of your suggestions.

588
00:24:44,000 --> 00:24:46,000
I know you're a proponent of the Scouts forming their own patrols,

589
00:24:46,000 --> 00:24:48,000
but how does this work out for less

590
00:24:48,000 --> 00:24:50,000
popular Scouts

591
00:24:50,000 --> 00:24:52,000
when a patrol or a patrol leader

592
00:24:52,000 --> 00:24:54,000
doesn't want a particular Scout in their patrol?

593
00:24:54,000 --> 00:24:56,000
do you have any particular

594
00:24:56,000 --> 00:24:58,000
ground rules set?

595
00:24:58,000 --> 00:25:00,000
well, we've talked about this

596
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:02,000
in the past couple of podcasts.

597
00:25:02,000 --> 00:25:04,000
the ground rules for everything that we do

598
00:25:04,000 --> 00:25:06,000
are the Scout Oath and Law.

599
00:25:06,000 --> 00:25:08,000
it's really the heart of the matter.

600
00:25:08,000 --> 00:25:10,000
no other rules are required.

601
00:25:10,000 --> 00:25:12,000
don't make other rules.

602
00:25:12,000 --> 00:25:14,000
champion the Scout Oath and Law.

603
00:25:14,000 --> 00:25:16,000
Scouts follow the Oath and Law.

604
00:25:16,000 --> 00:25:18,000
Scouts aren't concerned with popularity,

605
00:25:18,000 --> 00:25:20,000
serving their fellow Scouts.

606
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:22,000
so it's very difficult for me

607
00:25:22,000 --> 00:25:24,000
to imagine, in a situation

608
00:25:24,000 --> 00:25:26,000
where Scouts are following the Oath and Law,

609
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:28,000
that there's going to be a problem

610
00:25:28,000 --> 00:25:30,000
with leaving people out when they form patrols.

611
00:25:30,000 --> 00:25:32,000
now, boys,

612
00:25:32,000 --> 00:25:34,000
that's a different story.

613
00:25:34,000 --> 00:25:36,000
they need to think through what it means

614
00:25:36,000 --> 00:25:38,000
to be a Scout. so when

615
00:25:38,000 --> 00:25:40,000
the time comes to form patrols,

616
00:25:40,000 --> 00:25:42,000
this is something that you want to point out

617
00:25:42,000 --> 00:25:44,000
to your patrol leaders council.

618
00:25:44,000 --> 00:25:46,000
so a boy says: I don't want to be in a patrol

619
00:25:46,000 --> 00:25:48,000
with this other guy, he's a real pain.

620
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:50,000
well, my answer to something like that

621
00:25:50,000 --> 00:25:52,000
would be:

622
00:25:52,000 --> 00:25:54,000
maybe so,

623
00:25:54,000 --> 00:25:56,000
and I understand, because people can be

624
00:25:56,000 --> 00:25:58,000
a little bit of a pain sometimes.

625
00:25:58,000 --> 00:26:00,000
but what part of the Scout Oath and Law

626
00:26:00,000 --> 00:26:02,000
describes rejecting a fellow Scout

627
00:26:02,000 --> 00:26:04,000
because, quote, he's a pain?

628
00:26:04,000 --> 00:26:06,000
unquote.

629
00:26:06,000 --> 00:26:08,000
every boy gets to be a Scout

630
00:26:08,000 --> 00:26:10,000
and we accept him as he is

631
00:26:10,000 --> 00:26:12,000
because we practice the Scout Oath

632
00:26:12,000 --> 00:26:14,000
and Law.

633
00:26:14,000 --> 00:26:16,000
there are friendships between boys

634
00:26:16,000 --> 00:26:18,000
and that this is important.

635
00:26:18,000 --> 00:26:20,000
but a Scout is a friend to every other Scout

636
00:26:20,000 --> 00:26:22,000
and it doesn't necessarily mean

637
00:26:22,000 --> 00:26:24,000
that that Scout is their favorite.

638
00:26:24,000 --> 00:26:26,000
but if the Oath and Law mean anything at all,

639
00:26:26,000 --> 00:26:28,000
no Scout

640
00:26:28,000 --> 00:26:30,000
is certainly an enemy of any other Scout

641
00:26:30,000 --> 00:26:32,000
and no Scout is deemed

642
00:26:32,000 --> 00:26:34,000
unpopular.

643
00:26:34,000 --> 00:26:36,000
now, I know just as you do, the boys sometimes

644
00:26:36,000 --> 00:26:38,000
find it challenging to be Scouts.

645
00:26:38,000 --> 00:26:40,000
so it's our responsibility

646
00:26:40,000 --> 00:26:42,000
to champion the Oath and Law

647
00:26:42,000 --> 00:26:44,000
that we do.

648
00:26:44,000 --> 00:26:46,000
if you work at it, there's no questions about popularity.

649
00:26:46,000 --> 00:26:48,000
there's no room for marginalizing any Scout

650
00:26:48,000 --> 00:26:50,000
based on whether other

651
00:26:50,000 --> 00:26:52,000
boys like him or not.

652
00:26:52,000 --> 00:26:54,000
boys may do that kind of thing,

653
00:26:54,000 --> 00:26:56,000
but Scouts never do.

654
00:26:56,000 --> 00:26:58,000
Brad went on to ask: how often do

655
00:26:58,000 --> 00:27:00,000
your patrols rearrange?

656
00:27:00,000 --> 00:27:02,000
I think I remember you saying

657
00:27:02,000 --> 00:27:04,000
on one of the podcasts it could be often

658
00:27:04,000 --> 00:27:06,000
and at will. is that correct?

659
00:27:06,000 --> 00:27:08,000
how many distractions does that cause?

660
00:27:08,000 --> 00:27:10,000
well, Brad, our patrols get rearranged

661
00:27:10,000 --> 00:27:12,000
when the patrol leaders council think

662
00:27:12,000 --> 00:27:14,000
that it's needed.

663
00:27:14,000 --> 00:27:16,000
that's the at will part that you hear me talk about.

664
00:27:16,000 --> 00:27:18,000
the reset

665
00:27:18,000 --> 00:27:20,000
of patrols is usually spurred by the election

666
00:27:20,000 --> 00:27:22,000
a new senior patrol leader

667
00:27:22,000 --> 00:27:24,000
and then he's appointing Scouts

668
00:27:24,000 --> 00:27:26,000
to new positions of responsibility

669
00:27:26,000 --> 00:27:28,000
and sometimes that makes resetting

670
00:27:28,000 --> 00:27:30,000
the patrols

671
00:27:30,000 --> 00:27:32,000
necessary. now, in my experience,

672
00:27:32,000 --> 00:27:34,000
this causes pretty much zero distraction.

673
00:27:34,000 --> 00:27:36,000
every once in a while

674
00:27:36,000 --> 00:27:38,000
a Scout may want to shift from one patrol

675
00:27:38,000 --> 00:27:40,000
to another.

676
00:27:40,000 --> 00:27:42,000
they ask the senior patrol leader

677
00:27:42,000 --> 00:27:44,000
and I like to be made aware of those situations

678
00:27:44,000 --> 00:27:46,000
and give them a little advice.

679
00:27:46,000 --> 00:27:48,000
what I like to do is discuss the importance

680
00:27:48,000 --> 00:27:50,000
of patrol size related to function-

681
00:27:50,000 --> 00:27:52,000
not too big, not too small,

682
00:27:52,000 --> 00:27:54,000
and we've had happy patrols of four or five

683
00:27:54,000 --> 00:27:56,000
active Scouts and equally happy patrols of eight

684
00:27:56,000 --> 00:27:58,000
or ten. and overlaying all of this,

685
00:27:58,000 --> 00:28:00,000
as I said before,

686
00:28:00,000 --> 00:28:02,000
we talk about how the oath and law

687
00:28:02,000 --> 00:28:04,000
informs these decisions, and usually

688
00:28:04,000 --> 00:28:06,000
things come off without a hitch.

689
00:28:06,000 --> 00:28:08,000
here's two questions from the same source

690
00:28:08,000 --> 00:28:10,000
that asked to remain anonymous.

691
00:28:10,000 --> 00:28:12,000
question number one:

692
00:28:12,000 --> 00:28:14,000
I've been told as customary for Scoutmasters

693
00:28:14,000 --> 00:28:16,000
to step down from their role

694
00:28:16,000 --> 00:28:18,000
while their own son

695
00:28:18,000 --> 00:28:20,000
serves as senior patrol leader,

696
00:28:20,000 --> 00:28:22,000
which my son is currently doing.

697
00:28:22,000 --> 00:28:24,000
is this a common practice? I'm unaware of.

698
00:28:24,000 --> 00:28:26,000
I can remember sometime in the long,

699
00:28:26,000 --> 00:28:28,000
dim, distant past of hearing this

700
00:28:28,000 --> 00:28:30,000
somewhat obscure urban legend in Scouting.

701
00:28:30,000 --> 00:28:32,000
but no, it's not a common practice.

702
00:28:32,000 --> 00:28:34,000
if it was advisable

703
00:28:34,000 --> 00:28:36,000
or customary, it would be mentioned in our

704
00:28:36,000 --> 00:28:38,000
training or in the Scoutmaster Handbook

705
00:28:38,000 --> 00:28:40,000
or the troop committee guidebook

706
00:28:40,000 --> 00:28:42,000
or other resources that are provided to us.

707
00:28:42,000 --> 00:28:44,000
and I would ask whoever advised

708
00:28:44,000 --> 00:28:46,000
you to do that to point out where it's

709
00:28:46,000 --> 00:28:48,000
advised or mandated in the literature,

710
00:28:48,000 --> 00:28:50,000
because they won't find anything.

711
00:28:50,000 --> 00:28:52,000
question number two:

712
00:28:52,000 --> 00:28:54,000
a small faction of parents

713
00:28:54,000 --> 00:28:56,000
would be creating havoc for our troop committee.

714
00:28:56,000 --> 00:28:58,000
our district executive

715
00:28:58,000 --> 00:29:00,000
and unit commissioner have been

716
00:29:00,000 --> 00:29:02,000
in close contact with these parents,

717
00:29:02,000 --> 00:29:04,000
but they did not communicate the concerns

718
00:29:04,000 --> 00:29:06,000
that they had

719
00:29:06,000 --> 00:29:08,000
to us in a timely manner, and that complicated

720
00:29:08,000 --> 00:29:10,000
things further.

721
00:29:10,000 --> 00:29:12,000
is this a common practice?

722
00:29:12,000 --> 00:29:14,000
well, I really can't tell you

723
00:29:14,000 --> 00:29:16,000
if that's a common practice or not, but I've seen it

724
00:29:16,000 --> 00:29:18,000
before. it certainly seems to be

725
00:29:18,000 --> 00:29:20,000
kind of a counterproductive way of handling things

726
00:29:20,000 --> 00:29:22,000
I think anybody would rather hear about

727
00:29:22,000 --> 00:29:24,000
any issues or problems being raised

728
00:29:24,000 --> 00:29:26,000
right away, when

729
00:29:26,000 --> 00:29:28,000
possibly they could do something about it,

730
00:29:28,000 --> 00:29:30,000
rather than a couple of weeks or months

731
00:29:30,000 --> 00:29:32,000
later. these kinds of storms

732
00:29:32,000 --> 00:29:34,000
where parents get upset- maybe they talk to a

733
00:29:34,000 --> 00:29:36,000
commissioner or a district executive.

734
00:29:36,000 --> 00:29:38,000
these kind of things happen,

735
00:29:38,000 --> 00:29:40,000
hopefully rarely.

736
00:29:40,000 --> 00:29:42,000
I don't spend a whole lot of time

737
00:29:42,000 --> 00:29:44,000
worrying about them myself.

738
00:29:44,000 --> 00:29:46,000
I learned over years the hard way

739
00:29:46,000 --> 00:29:48,000
because I know how upsetting it is when this happens.

740
00:29:48,000 --> 00:29:50,000
if you just stick to your work

741
00:29:50,000 --> 00:29:52,000
as a Scoutmaster focused on

742
00:29:52,000 --> 00:29:54,000
delivering the program to your Scouts,

743
00:29:54,000 --> 00:29:56,000
things gain a little perspective.

744
00:29:56,000 --> 00:29:58,000
a lot of times storms like this come up

745
00:29:58,000 --> 00:30:00,000
when there's been some fundamental organizational change,

746
00:30:00,000 --> 00:30:02,000
like appointing a new Scoutmaster,

747
00:30:02,000 --> 00:30:04,000
and if you look at it from

748
00:30:04,000 --> 00:30:06,000
a positive perspective,

749
00:30:06,000 --> 00:30:08,000
when people are questioning what's going on,

750
00:30:08,000 --> 00:30:10,000
they're a good opportunity for a little introspection.

751
00:30:10,000 --> 00:30:12,000
you know,

752
00:30:12,000 --> 00:30:14,000
I ask what I'm doing and

753
00:30:14,000 --> 00:30:16,000
why I'm doing it and if I've carried out

754
00:30:16,000 --> 00:30:18,000
my responsibilities properly

755
00:30:18,000 --> 00:30:20,000
and whether or not I need to change my practices

756
00:30:20,000 --> 00:30:22,000
and attitudes. and when questions like this

757
00:30:22,000 --> 00:30:24,000
have come up in the past,

758
00:30:24,000 --> 00:30:26,000
they have certainly helped me do

759
00:30:26,000 --> 00:30:28,000
just that. Another anonymous question:

760
00:30:28,000 --> 00:30:30,000
our troop committee

761
00:30:30,000 --> 00:30:32,000
recently discussed a Scout

762
00:30:32,000 --> 00:30:34,000
who has been consistently spoken to

763
00:30:34,000 --> 00:30:36,000
about his disruptive behavior

764
00:30:36,000 --> 00:30:38,000
for the past six months.

765
00:30:38,000 --> 00:30:40,000
at our last camping trip

766
00:30:40,000 --> 00:30:42,000
he managed to bully two boys to the point

767
00:30:42,000 --> 00:30:44,000
where he made them cry.

768
00:30:44,000 --> 00:30:46,000
the Scoutmaster decided the Scout would be told

769
00:30:46,000 --> 00:30:48,000
he was taking a month long time out

770
00:30:48,000 --> 00:30:50,000
and the time that he had already served

771
00:30:50,000 --> 00:30:52,000
in a position of responsibility

772
00:30:52,000 --> 00:30:54,000
would not count towards

773
00:30:54,000 --> 00:30:56,000
his next rank. The committee disagreed

774
00:30:56,000 --> 00:30:58,000
with the Scoutmaster's judgment on this,

775
00:30:58,000 --> 00:31:00,000
but the Scoutmaster replied

776
00:31:00,000 --> 00:31:02,000
that this Scout was not living the oath

777
00:31:02,000 --> 00:31:04,000
and law and that's part of our expectations.

778
00:31:04,000 --> 00:31:06,000
I would appreciate your opinion

779
00:31:06,000 --> 00:31:08,000
on this situation.

780
00:31:08,000 --> 00:31:10,000
well, in my opinion,

781
00:31:10,000 --> 00:31:12,000
informed by the Scoutmaster handbook

782
00:31:12,000 --> 00:31:14,000
and training, in my experience

783
00:31:14,000 --> 00:31:16,000
Scoutmaster's troops and committees, or anyone

784
00:31:16,000 --> 00:31:18,000
other than the Scout's parents,

785
00:31:18,000 --> 00:31:20,000
should never discipline or punish

786
00:31:20,000 --> 00:31:22,000
Scouts, and this includes

787
00:31:22,000 --> 00:31:24,000
a month long time out you describe

788
00:31:24,000 --> 00:31:26,000
if a Scout is continually misbehaving-

789
00:31:26,000 --> 00:31:28,000
and to me

790
00:31:28,000 --> 00:31:30,000
continually is twice

791
00:31:30,000 --> 00:31:32,000
doing the same thing- after I've discussed it

792
00:31:32,000 --> 00:31:34,000
with him once,

793
00:31:34,000 --> 00:31:36,000
I speak to his parents.

794
00:31:36,000 --> 00:31:38,000
how we get six months into this

795
00:31:38,000 --> 00:31:40,000
without anybody taking any definitive

796
00:31:40,000 --> 00:31:42,000
action is part of the problem.

797
00:31:42,000 --> 00:31:44,000
if I've had to speak to a Scout

798
00:31:44,000 --> 00:31:46,000
about the same misbehavior

799
00:31:46,000 --> 00:31:48,000
more than once, his parents are going to get involved

800
00:31:48,000 --> 00:31:50,000
and I speak to the parents

801
00:31:50,000 --> 00:31:52,000
and I tell them that

802
00:31:52,000 --> 00:31:54,000
they need to address that at home because

803
00:31:54,000 --> 00:31:56,000
we don't discipline or punish Scouts

804
00:31:56,000 --> 00:31:58,000
and when they've taken whatever actions they consider

805
00:31:58,000 --> 00:32:00,000
adequate, for however long they think they need

806
00:32:00,000 --> 00:32:02,000
and can assure me

807
00:32:02,000 --> 00:32:04,000
that this sort of behavior won't be repeated,

808
00:32:04,000 --> 00:32:06,000
then, and only then,

809
00:32:06,000 --> 00:32:08,000
can their Scout return to being active

810
00:32:08,000 --> 00:32:10,000
with Scouts, and I go into this

811
00:32:10,000 --> 00:32:12,000
in a little more depth in a post called

812
00:32:12,000 --> 00:32:14,000
Constructive Scouting Discipline

813
00:32:14,000 --> 00:32:16,000
